The Security Council,
Reaffirming the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and recalling its relevant resolutions on the situation in the Middle East, including the Palestinian question, in particular resolutions 242 (1967), 338 (1973), 1397 (2002), 1515 (2003), 1850 (2008), 2334 (2016), 2712 (2023), 2720 (2023), 2728 (2024) and 2735 (2024),
Recalling that resolution 2735 (2024) welcomed the ceasefire proposal announced on 31 May 2024 and underlined that the implementation of that proposal would enable an immediate, full and complete ceasefire with the release of hostages, the withdrawal of Israeli forces from populated areas in Gaza, the return of Palestinian civilians to their homes and neighbourhoods in all areas of Gaza, and the safe and effective distribution of humanitarian assistance at scale throughout the Gaza Strip,
Gravely concerned by the catastrophic humanitarian situation in the Gaza Strip, including the loss of tens of thousands of civilian lives, the widespread destruction of civilian infrastructure, the displacement of the overwhelming majority of the population, the risk of famine, and the collapse of health, water, sanitation and education services,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing military operations in Gaza, including strikes on or near schools, shelters, hospitals, humanitarian distribution points and United Nations premises, and at the killing and injury of civilians, including women and children, humanitarian workers, medical personnel, journalists and United Nations personnel,
Condemning all attacks against civilians, including the attacks of 7 October 2023, and all acts of hostage-taking, and demanding the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages and persons arbitrarily detained,
Expressing grave concern at restrictions on humanitarian access, the closure of crossings, the obstruction of relief convoys, and the dangerous conditions faced by humanitarian personnel, and stressing that humanitarian assistance must be provided in accordance with the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,
Reaffirming the obligation of all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the principles of distinction, proportionality and precautions in attack, and the prohibition of starvation as a method of warfare,
Rejecting any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that reduce the territory of Gaza or forcibly displace its population, and recalling that the Gaza Strip constitutes an integral part of the Palestinian territory occupied in 1967 and must be unified with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority,
Reaffirming its unwavering commitment to the vision of the two-State solution, where two democratic States, Israel and Palestine, live side by side in peace within secure and recognized borders, consistent with international law and relevant United Nations resolutions,
Stressing the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, and for the protection of humanitarian personnel, United Nations premises and diplomatic personnel,
Welcoming the diplomatic efforts of Egypt, Qatar and the United States of America, and other regional and international efforts aimed at reaching a comprehensive ceasefire and addressing the humanitarian crisis,
Expressing grave concern at settlement expansion and at acts of violence against civilians in the occupied West Bank, including East Jerusalem, and underscoring the need to refrain from unilateral actions that undermine the prospects for peace,
1. Demands the immediate implementation of resolution 2735 (2024) and of the ceasefire proposal without delay, including an immediate, full and complete ceasefire, the release of all hostages, the exchange of Palestinian prisoners, the withdrawal of Israeli forces from populated areas in Gaza, the return of Palestinian civilians to their homes and neighbourhoods in all areas of Gaza, and the safe and effective distribution of humanitarian assistance at scale throughout the Gaza Strip;
2. Demands the immediate and unconditional release of all hostages and persons arbitrarily detained, and calls for the dignified return of the remains of deceased hostages to their families, in accordance with international humanitarian law;
3. Demands that all parties comply with international humanitarian law and human rights law, protect civilians and civilian objects, refrain from indiscriminate and disproportionate attacks, and cease attacks on schools, hospitals, shelters, humanitarian distribution points, United Nations premises, humanitarian workers, medical personnel and journalists;
4. Demands the immediate, safe, unhindered and sustained delivery of humanitarian assistance at scale to all parts of the Gaza Strip, including the north, and in this regard demands the opening of all crossings, the facilitation of relief convoys, the removal of restrictions and obstacles to humanitarian access, the restoration of essential services, and the protection of humanitarian personnel and infrastructure;
5. Requests the Secretary-General to strengthen the United Nations humanitarian response in Gaza, including through increased coordination with humanitarian agencies and partners, and encourages Member States to provide adequate and predictable humanitarian funding;
6. Demands that all parties take immediate steps to ensure the protection of civilians seeking humanitarian assistance, and stresses the need for accountability for all acts of violence against them;
7. Rejects any attempt at demographic or territorial change in the Gaza Strip, including any actions that reduce the territory of Gaza, demands that no Palestinian civilian be forcibly displaced, and demands that all displaced Palestinians be allowed to return to their homes and neighbourhoods in all areas of Gaza, including the north;
8. Calls upon all parties to refrain from unilateral actions that undermine the two-State solution, including settlement expansion, demolitions, evictions and acts of violence against civilians, and underscores the need for full respect for international law in the occupied Palestinian territory, including East Jerusalem;
9. Reaffirms that the Gaza Strip is an integral part of the Palestinian territory occupied in 1967 and stresses the importance of unifying the Gaza Strip with the West Bank under the Palestinian Authority;
10. Calls for the resumption of negotiations and diplomatic efforts under the auspices of Egypt, Qatar, the United States of America and other partners to reach a comprehensive ceasefire and a just, lasting and comprehensive peace, and urges all parties to engage in good faith;
11. Stresses the need for accountability for all violations of international humanitarian law and human rights law, and requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council within 30 days on the implementation of this resolution, including on humanitarian access, the protection of civilians and compliance with international law;
12. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

